WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
Sprinkle baking soda into hydrochloric acid, and it

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.240
fizzes, and the solution becomes less acidic.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.840
Now sprinkle it into sodium hydroxide solution.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:12.960
No fizz this time, but the solution becomes

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:13.920
less basic.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.960
The same white powder calms an acid and

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.200
calms a base.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.320
So how can one substance act as a

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.800
base in one reaction, and as an acid

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:23.520
in another?

00:00:24.734 --> 00:00:28.013
Start with an equation you already know: ammonia

00:00:28.174 --> 00:00:28.893
in water.

00:00:29.454 --> 00:00:32.653
Water gives a proton to ammonia, making ammonium

00:00:32.814 --> 00:00:34.013
and hydroxide.

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:35.773
Now run it backwards.

00:00:36.014 --> 00:00:39.053
Ammonium can give that proton back, so it's

00:00:39.094 --> 00:00:39.613
an acid.

00:00:40.094 --> 00:00:42.893
Hydroxide can take it, so it's a base.

00:00:43.534 --> 00:00:46.333
Every acid-base reaction makes a new acid and

00:00:46.414 --> 00:00:48.813
a new base, ready to swap the proton

00:00:48.894 --> 00:00:49.293
back.

00:00:49.774 --> 00:00:52.413
The proton keeps swapping both ways, which is

00:00:52.494 --> 00:00:54.733
why this equation has the double arrow.

00:00:55.934 --> 00:00:58.013
Each new species is linked to the one

00:00:58.094 --> 00:00:58.893
it came from.

00:00:59.374 --> 00:01:01.973
When an acid gives away its proton, what's

00:01:02.014 --> 00:01:04.093
left is its conjugate base.

00:01:04.334 --> 00:01:06.653
When a base takes a proton, it becomes

00:01:06.734 --> 00:01:08.093
its conjugate acid.

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:10.053
Conjugate means joined.

00:01:10.254 --> 00:01:13.773
So ammonium and ammonia are one conjugate pair,

00:01:13.854 --> 00:01:16.093
and water and hydroxide are the other.

00:01:16.494 --> 00:01:19.613
Partners differ by exactly one proton, so the

00:01:19.814 --> 00:01:22.093
charge changes by one, and they sit on

00:01:22.174 --> 00:01:23.853
opposite sides of the equation.

00:01:25.067 --> 00:01:28.866
Try a harder one: hydrogen phosphate in water.

00:01:29.227 --> 00:01:32.106
H P O four two minus, plus water,

00:01:32.187 --> 00:01:34.746
gives H two P O four minus, plus

00:01:34.947 --> 00:01:35.826
hydroxide.

00:01:36.107 --> 00:01:37.306
Follow the proton.

00:01:37.707 --> 00:01:41.226
Water loses one and becomes hydroxide, so water

00:01:41.307 --> 00:01:43.786
is the acid, and hydroxide is its conjugate

00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:44.346
base.

00:01:44.827 --> 00:01:48.106
Hydrogen phosphate gains one, so it's the base,

00:01:48.187 --> 00:01:50.986
and dihydrogen phosphate is its conjugate acid.

00:01:51.387 --> 00:01:54.666
Always one proton per pair: the conjugate base

00:01:54.707 --> 00:01:56.746
of H two S O four is H

00:01:56.827 --> 00:01:59.386
S O four minus, not sulfate.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.080
Pairs also explain the arrows from the strength

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:03.840
video.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:05.640
Look at the conjugate bases.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.800
Chloride, from hydrogen chloride, has almost no pull

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:12.520
on a proton, so it almost never takes

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:13.360
one back.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.960
That's why the reaction goes all the way,

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.080
with a single arrow.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.160
Acetate, from acetic acid, has a real pull,

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.360
so it keeps taking protons back from hydronium,

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.280
and you get the double arrow.

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:28.680
So the stronger the acid, the weaker its

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:29.720
conjugate base.

00:02:30.934 --> 00:02:32.413
Now the baking soda.

00:02:32.694 --> 00:02:34.853
The ion that does the work is hydrogen

00:02:34.934 --> 00:02:38.013
carbonate, H C O three minus.

00:02:38.294 --> 00:02:40.293
In the acid, it takes a proton from

00:02:40.374 --> 00:02:44.053
hydronium, making carbonic acid, which breaks down into

00:02:44.134 --> 00:02:46.933
water and the carbon dioxide you saw fizzing.

00:02:47.174 --> 00:02:49.653
In the sodium hydroxide, it gives a proton

00:02:49.734 --> 00:02:53.173
to hydroxide, making carbonate and water, so there's

00:02:53.254 --> 00:02:53.893
no gas.

00:02:54.454 --> 00:02:56.453
A species that can give or take a

00:02:56.534 --> 00:02:59.973
proton, depending on its partner, is amphiprotic.

00:03:00.534 --> 00:03:03.733
Amphi means both, like an amphibian, at home

00:03:03.814 --> 00:03:05.493
on land and in water.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:08.020
How do you spot one?

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.780
Line the species up in a proton chain,

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:12.740
each one proton apart.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:16.300
Carbonic acid, hydrogen carbonate, carbonate.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.580
Phosphoric acid loses its three protons one at

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:24.860
a time: dihydrogen phosphate, hydrogen phosphate, then phosphate.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:28.060
The ends do one job: phosphoric acid can

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:31.180
only give, and phosphate can only take.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:33.260
The species in the middle can still give

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:35.820
a proton and can also take one, so

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:37.500
they're amphiprotic.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.700
Even hydrogen phosphate, with its two minus charge,

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:42.460
still has a proton to give.

00:03:43.667 --> 00:03:45.746
Exam questions ask you to show this with

00:03:45.827 --> 00:03:49.106
equations, often for sodium hydrogen carbonate.

00:03:49.747 --> 00:03:52.546
First it dissolves into sodium ions, which don't

00:03:52.627 --> 00:03:55.666
react, and hydrogen carbonate ions.

00:03:55.987 --> 00:03:58.626
Then write one equation with water for each

00:03:58.707 --> 00:03:59.426
role.

00:03:59.747 --> 00:04:02.706
As an acid, hydrogen carbonate gives a proton

00:04:02.787 --> 00:04:06.066
to water, making carbonate and hydronium.

00:04:06.147 --> 00:04:08.386
As a base, it takes a proton from

00:04:08.467 --> 00:04:11.986
water, making carbonic acid and hydroxide.

00:04:12.147 --> 00:04:15.506
Both are partial, so both get double arrows.

00:04:15.627 --> 00:04:18.306
In baking soda solution the base reaction goes

00:04:18.387 --> 00:04:21.106
slightly further, which is why baking soda makes

00:04:21.307 --> 00:04:24.066
water mildly basic, as the models video said.

00:04:25.267 --> 00:04:26.946
Water sits in the middle of its own

00:04:27.027 --> 00:04:30.226
chain: hydronium, water, hydroxide.

00:04:30.787 --> 00:04:33.506
So water is amphiprotic too, and it even

00:04:33.587 --> 00:04:35.266
reacts with itself.

00:04:35.347 --> 00:04:38.066
One water molecule gives a proton to another,

00:04:38.147 --> 00:04:41.386
making a hydronium ion and a hydroxide ion.

00:04:41.827 --> 00:04:44.866
Very pure water still conducts a tiny current,

00:04:45.027 --> 00:04:46.706
because these ions are there.

00:04:46.827 --> 00:04:49.906
But it's a tiny equilibrium: only about two

00:04:49.987 --> 00:04:52.146
water molecules in a billion have given a

00:04:52.227 --> 00:04:54.306
proton away at any moment.

00:04:54.387 --> 00:04:56.786
And because each transfer makes one of each,

00:04:56.827 --> 00:04:59.266
they're always in equal numbers.

00:04:59.427 --> 00:05:02.226
Equal amounts of hydronium and hydroxide is what

00:05:02.307 --> 00:05:05.106
neutral means, and the next video links that

00:05:05.187 --> 00:05:06.306
to pH seven.

00:05:07.534 --> 00:05:09.053
So here's what to remember.

00:05:09.534 --> 00:05:13.293
A conjugate pair differs by exactly one proton:

00:05:13.414 --> 00:05:15.613
take one off for the conjugate base, add

00:05:15.694 --> 00:05:17.133
one for the conjugate acid.

00:05:17.614 --> 00:05:20.333
Amphi means both: the middle of a proton

00:05:20.414 --> 00:05:22.093
chain can go either way.

00:05:22.334 --> 00:05:24.573
And the stronger the acid, the weaker its

00:05:24.654 --> 00:05:25.773
conjugate base.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:27.560
Your turn.

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:31.480
Potassium dihydrogen phosphate is used in foods to

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:33.240
control acidity.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.200
Write an equation for it dissolving in water.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:39.240
Then write two equations, each with water as

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:42.200
the partner, to show that the dihydrogen phosphate

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:44.440
ion is amphiprotic.

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:45.800
Pause here to answer.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:59.960
It dissolves into potassium ions and dihydrogen phosphate

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:00.800
ions.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:04.360
As an acid, dihydrogen phosphate gives a proton

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:07.960
to water, making hydrogen phosphate and hydronium.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:10.760
As a base, it takes a proton from

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:14.280
water, making phosphoric acid and hydroxide.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:17.400
If you wrote phosphate, P O four three

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:20.640
minus, you moved two steps along the chain.

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:21.880
Move just one.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:23.740
Last one.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:27.820
The equation shows ammonium ions reacting with carbonate

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:28.460
ions.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:31.820
Identify the two conjugate acid-base pairs in this

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:35.420
reaction, and state which species is the Brønsted-Lowry

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:36.300
acid.

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:37.660
Pause here to answer.

00:06:48.067 --> 00:06:49.546
Follow the proton.

00:06:49.827 --> 00:06:54.146
Ammonium loses one and becomes ammonia, so ammonium

00:06:54.307 --> 00:06:57.266
is the acid, and ammonium and ammonia are

00:06:57.347 --> 00:06:58.306
one pair.

00:06:58.627 --> 00:07:02.306
Carbonate gains one and becomes hydrogen carbonate, so

00:07:02.387 --> 00:07:03.546
that's the other pair.

00:07:03.827 --> 00:07:07.346
Pairing ammonium with carbonate is the classic slip:

00:07:07.387 --> 00:07:09.346
they're on the same side, and they don't

00:07:09.427 --> 00:07:10.946
differ by one proton.

00:07:12.167 --> 00:07:15.846
So for any acid-base equation, follow the proton,

00:07:15.927 --> 00:07:18.406
then pair each species with its partner one

00:07:18.487 --> 00:07:21.046
proton away on the other side, and check

00:07:21.087 --> 00:07:22.966
the charge changes by one.

00:07:23.287 --> 00:07:26.246
For an amphiprotic ion, write one equation giving

00:07:26.327 --> 00:07:28.766
a proton to water, and one taking a

00:07:28.807 --> 00:07:30.086
proton from water.

00:07:30.807 --> 00:07:34.486
Next: putting a number on water's tiny equilibrium,

00:07:34.567 --> 00:07:36.646
and using it to calculate pH.

00:07:37.867 --> 00:07:39.306
Here are the key notes.

00:07:39.547 --> 00:07:41.466
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:41.547 --> 00:07:42.346
to copy them.
